1
00:00:11,850 --> 00:00:12,850
Hello, everybody.

2
00:00:13,690 --> 00:00:16,890
Welcome back to IELTS Intermediate Speaking Course by PREP.

3
00:00:17,430 --> 00:00:18,610
Welcome to Lesson 5.

4
00:00:19,050 --> 00:00:23,910
We are finally tackling the last challenge of the IELTS Speaking Test, Part 3.

5
00:00:24,630 --> 00:00:28,250
To understand how this part works, let's look at three questions, which

6
00:00:28,250 --> 00:00:31,430
represent three different parts of the IELTS Speaking Test.

7
00:00:31,430 --> 00:00:35,390
One, do you prefer studying alone or with a teacher?

8
00:00:36,170 --> 00:00:39,670
Two, describe a teacher who has greatly influenced you.

9
00:00:40,130 --> 00:00:45,210
Three, how has the relationship between teachers and students changed in recent years?

10
00:00:45,970 --> 00:00:48,450
They are all about the same topic, education.

11
00:00:49,290 --> 00:00:52,110
But do you know which part each question belongs to?

12
00:00:52,610 --> 00:00:55,810
Or more importantly, can you see the key difference between them?

13
00:00:56,350 --> 00:01:01,730
Let's learn about how questions in Part 3 are different from the other two in today's lesson.

14
00:01:02,410 --> 00:01:09,250
This lesson aims to help you, one, learn about the format and requirements of IELTS Speaking Part 3.

15
00:01:09,950 --> 00:01:14,390
Two, learn about how to achieve a higher score in this part of the test.

16
00:01:15,070 --> 00:01:16,110
Now let's go.

17
00:01:21,220 --> 00:01:24,080
Now let's look at the first key aspect of Part 3.

18
00:01:24,780 --> 00:01:27,180
Where do Part 3 questions actually come from?

19
00:01:27,700 --> 00:01:33,100
Some of you also might think, surely the examiner just picks some random topics for Part 3.

20
00:01:33,600 --> 00:01:35,460
However, the answer is no.

21
00:01:36,120 --> 00:01:40,240
This is because, in almost every test for Part 3, the examiner

22
00:01:40,240 --> 00:01:44,100
will ask you questions related to the topic you discussed in Part 2.

23
00:01:44,860 --> 00:01:47,940
Yet we will also move away from personal stories of this part

24
00:01:47,940 --> 00:01:51,880
and step into the world of abstract concepts for Part 3.

25
00:01:52,500 --> 00:01:55,580
Let's look at how this transformation happens with real topics.

26
00:01:56,380 --> 00:02:00,200
After you spent two minutes describing a teacher who has greatly influenced

27
00:02:00,200 --> 00:02:04,780
you, then in Part 3, the examiner might ask about the changes

28
00:02:04,780 --> 00:02:08,340
in the relationship between teachers and students in recent years.

29
00:02:09,060 --> 00:02:10,000
Can you notice the pattern?

30
00:02:10,640 --> 00:02:15,320
We will stop saying, my teacher, my school, I think, and start

31
00:02:15,320 --> 00:02:19,400
saying, students today, the education system, society.

32
00:02:20,100 --> 00:02:22,740
This is the shift from the personal to the abstract.

33
00:02:23,400 --> 00:02:25,820
This part is built to test your higher level skills.

34
00:02:26,540 --> 00:02:29,280
The examiner wants to know whether you are able to deal with

35
00:02:29,280 --> 00:02:35,340
unfamiliar topics with greater difficulty, unlike the questions that you face in Part 1 and Part 2.

36
00:02:36,060 --> 00:02:39,700
Because it demands a high level of depth, most candidates agree that

37
00:02:39,700 --> 00:02:43,660
this is the toughest challenge in the entire exam, instead of Part 2.

38
00:02:44,320 --> 00:02:47,800
But with the right structure and test awareness for this part, it

39
00:02:47,800 --> 00:02:51,060
is a hurdle that you definitely can clear to achieve a higher score.

40
00:02:55,680 --> 00:03:02,540
So before we look at specific answer structures, let's systematize the core rules you need to succeed in Part 3.

41
00:03:03,120 --> 00:03:05,040
First, the depth of your response.

42
00:03:05,880 --> 00:03:07,720
You must attempt to extend your responses.

43
00:03:08,780 --> 00:03:10,460
Short answers here will lower your score.

44
00:03:11,120 --> 00:03:14,200
You might ask, why are short answers such a problem here?

45
00:03:14,720 --> 00:03:15,700
The reason is simple.

46
00:03:16,280 --> 00:03:20,440
This section is designed to evaluate your ability to express complex ideas,

47
00:03:21,060 --> 00:03:24,200
justify opinions, or speculate about future trends.

48
00:03:24,980 --> 00:03:30,000
If you give a short and underdeveloped answer, you are showing that you cannot demonstrate these skills.

49
00:03:30,600 --> 00:03:33,460
The examiner is trying to see how much you can explore the

50
00:03:33,460 --> 00:03:37,680
topic, so providing depth is the only way to prove you deserve a high score.

51
00:03:38,600 --> 00:03:44,240
However, please remember that an optimal length for a Part 3 answer is from 30 seconds to a minute.

52
00:03:45,140 --> 00:03:48,900
Longer answers sometimes will lead to grammatical issues or fluency problems,

53
00:03:49,280 --> 00:03:51,620
so remember not to overextend your response.

54
00:03:52,480 --> 00:03:54,340
Second, let's talk about the topics.

55
00:03:54,980 --> 00:03:59,120
You might worry, do I need to be an expert to answer these abstract questions?

56
00:03:59,660 --> 00:04:00,900
The answer is no.

57
00:04:01,760 --> 00:04:06,220
Most Part 3 topics are about universal issues, things that affect everyone's

58
00:04:06,220 --> 00:04:09,380
life like technology, education, or shopping.

59
00:04:10,140 --> 00:04:15,779
They are designed this way so that every candidate, regardless of their job or major, has something to say.

60
00:04:16,380 --> 00:04:21,120
Here, the examiner is testing your ability to share your perspective as a member of society.

61
00:04:21,940 --> 00:04:27,120
Whether you are a high school student or a CEO, you both experience traffic or advertising.

62
00:04:27,800 --> 00:04:30,420
That's why you just need to explain your own view on it.

63
00:04:31,100 --> 00:04:36,700
Remember, most of you cannot know about everything, and also, this part does not aim to test your knowledge.

64
00:04:37,620 --> 00:04:41,120
Finally, it is about interaction between you and your examiner.

65
00:04:41,800 --> 00:04:47,160
The IELTS speaking test is face-to-face, and you have a human examiner who wants to hear you speak.

66
00:04:47,820 --> 00:04:51,140
They will actively encourage you to keep talking and explore the topic.

67
00:04:51,800 --> 00:04:54,660
Because of this, they can also help you if you get stuck.

68
00:04:55,300 --> 00:04:58,420
If they use a difficult word you don't know, do not guess.

69
00:04:59,240 --> 00:05:00,960
The rules allow you to ask for the meaning.

70
00:05:01,460 --> 00:05:03,580
The examiner will explain the term to you.

71
00:05:04,040 --> 00:05:07,940
Doing this ensures you understand the question and keeps your answer on topic.

72
00:05:08,620 --> 00:05:11,180
Don't hesitate to ask for an explanation at any point.

73
00:05:11,540 --> 00:05:13,560
It shows you are engaged in the conversation.

74
00:05:20,460 --> 00:05:24,900
That wraps up our Introduction to IELTS Speaking Part 3, which focuses

75
00:05:24,900 --> 00:05:30,160
on wider topics and more abstract concepts than the previous two parts of the test.

76
00:05:30,940 --> 00:05:34,760
We also established the three golden insights for a high score.

77
00:05:35,660 --> 00:05:37,860
One, the depth of Part 3 response.

78
00:05:38,620 --> 00:05:44,880
You must extend your answers to express complex ideas, but keep them between 30 to 60 seconds.

79
00:05:45,500 --> 00:05:47,260
Two, universal topics.

80
00:05:47,880 --> 00:05:49,900
Remember that you don't need expert knowledge.

81
00:05:50,260 --> 00:05:55,880
You just need to share your perspective on issues that affect society or even your daily life.

82
00:05:56,740 --> 00:05:57,720
Three, interaction.

83
00:05:58,740 --> 00:06:04,640
Don't just answer, engage with the examiner, and don't hesitate to ask for clarification if needed.

84
00:06:06,220 --> 00:06:12,820
Study the lessons, do the exercises regularly, and make the most of yourself study whenever you have some free time.

85
00:06:13,480 --> 00:06:14,780
Till next time, bye!

